Summary: This lesson introduces a way to populate a website with data obtained from another website via an Application Programming Interface (API). Using some simple programming, it provides strategies for customizing the presentation of that data, providing flexible and generalizable skills.

Scope:
-	APIs are frequently used as a means of accessing or distributing Open Data (data that is accessible to anyone to use, reuse, and redistribute without restrictions)
-	APIs are often tailored for developers, and it is still not easy for the researchers with little technical experience to work on them
-	In the tutorial, we learn basic HTML (Hypertext Markup Language – which is standard code used to structure and display content on the internet, like the skeleton of every website) and PHP (Hypertext Preprocessor – a open-source server-side scripting language explicitly designed for web development) to build a simple website to display API query results of cultural heritage collections, using Europeana API
-	Examples from this tutorial uses some basic programming, however it is usually also possible to use copy and paste when working with API at a basic level

Contents:
-	Tutorial is broken up into two parts:
-	Part one consists of:
o	What is API? And a brief history of the web
o	Why is it useful?
-	Part two consists of more practical application:
o	Registering for a Europeana API key
o	Viewing Europeana API data on a web browser
o	Installing XAMPP
o	Using a local web server and creating a web page with PHP and HTML
o	Developing a web page for a Europeana API with PHP and HTML
