What is Application Programming Interface (API)?
-	Internet Story to Far:
o	The World Wide Web (WWW) was initially designed for human-to-machine communication, as we humans created websites on our computers, and other humans sat them on their computers by using web browsers
o	At the time, were mostly static web pages because they were documents and the contents were fixed by webmasters. Could passively view somebody’s web pages with texts and photos with a Graphical User Interface (GUI) – with the interaction being mostly between humans and machines
o	Then web pages became dynamic as humans could interact with each other, as content was not fixed by dynamically changes by the actions of users, such as social media where we not only consume web content but also generate it
o	In order to manage such web resources, we needed database systems behind websites, so that we could store and manage data properly and efficiently
o	Realized we needed more machine-to-machine communication and as we have more data than humans can actually browse or work on, we needed methods for machines to smoothly communicate with each other, which resulted in the creation of a Web Service – which APIs essentially are
o	Not only can machines request and obtain data, but also process data they submit – for this tutorial, we focus on obtaining useful data for research purposes
o	An example of this is imagine running a ski website and you want weather updates for the mountain every half hour, you receive the forecast form a meteorological website for your ski fields every half an hour. It is easier to build a website which can automatically fetch he weather data and simply them at regular intervals. This automation is possible with APIs
o	APIs normally offer standardized raw data and access methods, allowing you to manipulate and customize data on your end- such as converting Fahrenheit to Celsius or converting a line chart in place of a bar chart
o	Websites can offer both web pages and APIs, such as Wikipedia offering APIs for other users to let them develop an app, which uses Wikipedia’s machine-readable raw data
-	Why are APIs Useful for Researchers?
o	Researchers may be intersetd to use:	
	More data
	Related data
	Interlinking data
	Remote data
	Up-to-date data
	Different types of data
	Data with better quality
	Data not obtainable
	Data with a low price
o	In general, researchers are keen to take advantage of the power of the Internet by sharing and reusing data
o	Therefore, “Open-Data” and “date-driven research” have been under the spotlight of academia and industries for the last several years, with APIs being one of the growing areas of this movement
o	By expanding what datasets are accessible, we can do much more than we could before, leading to change in research practices with hopefully more discoveries and innovations

